--TEST--
Spec § 4: `Core\Time::fromIso` refuses a well-formed timestamp past either end of the timestamp range as a `ParseError` with one sentence of its own naming the range, and never the calendar library's parameter name, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Time::fromIso
// The first refused second at each end, written in UTC and with an offset.
// All four throw the same sentence, so the text is printed once and the
// sentences are counted for agreement.
array<string> $past = ["9999-12-30T22:00:01Z", "9999-12-31T23:59:59+01:00", "-009999-01-02T01:59:58Z", "-009999-01-01T00:00:00-01:00"];
array<string> $sentences = [];
foreach ($past as string $text) {
    try {
        var $at = Core\Time::fromIso($text);
        $sentences[] = "accepted " . $at->toIso();
    } catch (ParseError $outside) {
        $sentences[] = $outside->message;
    }
}
echo $sentences[0], "\n";
int $same = 0;
foreach ($sentences as string $sentence) {
    if ($sentence == $sentences[0]) {
        $same = $same + 1;
    }
}
echo $same as string, " of 4 agree\n";

// The last accepted second at each end, beside them, still builds.
echo Core\Time::fromIso("9999-12-30T22:00:00Z")->toIso(), "|", Core\Time::fromIso("-009999-01-02T01:59:59Z")->toIso(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Time::fromIso(): the time is outside -9999-01-02T01:59:59Z..=9999-12-30T22:00:00Z
4 of 4 agree
9999-12-30T22:00:00Z|-009999-01-02T01:59:59Z
